-- title:
--   Formal quotient datum from a Mumford tower and a tower quotient
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $r$. The arithmetic data consist of: a characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O$ which is a discrete valuation ring (hypothesis `hdvr`) with an irreducible element $\pi$, $\pi$-adically complete, whose residue ring $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ has exactly $r$ elements and in which $(r)=(\pi)$ (so $\mathcal O$ is absolutely unramified with residue field $\mathbb F_r$); and a characteristic-zero field $K_0$ which is the fraction field of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   The unramified coefficients consist of a characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ over $\mathcal O$ together with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, subject to: $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$, that ideal is maximal, every element of $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial of positive degree over $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`, algebraic closedness of the residue field), and $\mathrm{Fr}(x)\equiv x^{r}$ modulo $\pi$ for all $x$ (`hFr`, so $\mathrm{Fr}$ is a Frobenius lift).
--
--   A homomorphism $v\!\det : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)\to\mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) is given, normalised by `hvdet`: $v\!\det(g)=n$ if and only if $\det g = u\,\pi^{n}$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   The group data consist of a group $G$, a homomorphism $\sigma: G\to \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$, and subgroups $\Gamma,\Gamma'$ with: `hcent`, some $z\in\Gamma$ has $\sigma z$ a scalar matrix with $v\!\det(\sigma z)=2$; `hodd`, some $w\in\Gamma$ has $v\!\det(\sigma w)=1$; and `hΓ'`, $\Gamma'$ is exactly the set of $x\in\Gamma$ with $v\!\det(\sigma x)$ even. Further, $\rho: G\to \mathrm{PGL}_2(K_0)$ is the projectivisation of $\sigma$ (`hρ`), and the image $\rho(\Gamma')$ acts on the vertices of the lattice tree of $\mathcal O\subset K_0$ (homothety classes of full lattices) with finite vertex stabilisers (`hdisc`) and with finitely many orbits, in the form of a finite set $S$ of vertices meeting every orbit (`hcocpt`). The element $g_1\in\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ is the diagonal matrix $\mathrm{diag}(\pi,1)$.
--
--   A subgroup $N\le \mathrm{PGL}_2(K_0)$ is given with $N\le\rho(\Gamma')$, with $N\cap\rho(\Gamma)$ normal in $\rho(\Gamma)$, and with non-zero (i.e. finite) relative index in $\rho(\Gamma')$, together with a term `DM : MumfordTower 𝒪 π K₀ r g₁ N`: a tower of schemes $Z_n$ with proper flat structure maps $zb_n$ to $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal O/\pi^{n+1})$, cartesian transition maps $zt_n$, affine neighbourhoods of finite subsets, and a family of maps $q^{M}_n$ sending a Deligne datum over a $\pi^{n+1}$-torsion $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ to a $B$-point of $Z_n$, compatible with the base, natural in $B$, compatible with the transitions, invariant under pullback along elements of $N$, and satisfying the chart conditions of that structure. (Here a Deligne datum over $B$, the value at $B$ of the functor `Omega K₀ π`, assigns to every full $\mathcal O$-lattice $M\subset K_0^2$ a $B$-submodule of $B\otimes_{\mathcal O} M$ with invertible quotient, monotone in $M$, equivariant for homotheties, and non-degenerate at every prime of $B$.)
--
--   The twisted tower consists of schemes $X_n$ with structure maps $xb_n$ to $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal O/\pi^{n+1})$ and transition maps $xt_n$, a finite group $G_2$ acting by $a_n : G_2\to\operatorname{Aut}(X_n)$, projections $pr^1_n : X_n\to Z_n$ and $pr^2_n : X_n \to \operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal O_2/\pi^{n+1})$, where $\mathcal O_2$ denotes the equaliser of $\mathrm{Fr}\circ\mathrm{Fr}$ and the identity on $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$, that is $\{y : \mathrm{Fr}(\mathrm{Fr}\,y)=y\}$; a homomorphism $\theta:\Gamma\to G_2$; and $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphisms $\mathrm{Fr}_{2,n}$ of $\mathcal O_2/\pi^{n+1}$. These are subject to: cartesianness of the transition squares (`hcart`), properness and flatness of the $xb_n$ (`hproper`, `hflat`), existence of affine opens containing any finite subset (`haff`), compatibility of the $G_2$-action with the base maps and the transitions (`ha_over`, `ha_xt`), cartesianness of the square expressing $X_n$ as the fibre product of $Z_n$ and $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal O_2/\pi^{n+1})$ over $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal O/\pi^{n+1})$ (`hX`), the identity $xb_n = pr^1_n$ followed by $zb_n$ (`hxb`), compatibility of $pr^1$, $pr^2$ with the transitions (`hxt₁`, `hxt₂`), surjectivity of $\theta$ and the identification of its kernel, $\theta\gamma=1$ iff $\rho(\gamma)\in N$ (`hθsurj`, `hθker`), the description of $\mathrm{Fr}_{2,n}$ as induced by $\mathrm{Fr}$ on classes (`hFr₂`), the rule that for $\gamma\in\Gamma$ the automorphism $a_n(\theta\gamma)$ followed by $pr^2_n$ equals $pr^2_n$ followed by $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\mathrm{Fr}_{2,n}^{-v\!\det(\sigma\gamma)}$ (`ha_pr₂`), and the rule `ha_pr₁`: for $\gamma\in\Gamma$, a $\pi^{n+1}$-torsion $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ and Deligne data $P,P'$ over $B$ with $P'$ the pullback of $P$ along $(\sigma\gamma)^{-1}$, any $B$-point $x$ of $X_n$ with $x$ followed by $pr^1_n$ equal to $q^{M}_n(P)$ satisfies that $x$ followed by $a_n(\theta\gamma)$ and then $pr^1_n$ equals $q^{M}_n(P')$.
--
--   Finally a term `DQ : TowerQuotientDatum 𝒪 π X xb xt G₂ a` is given: a tower $Y_n$ over $\mathcal O/\pi^{n+1}$ with cartesian transitions, proper and flat base maps, and finite surjective $G_2$-invariant projections $p_n : X_n\to Y_n$ which are cartesian over the transitions, epimorphic over each open, and satisfy the local universal property of the quotient, together with the remaining axioms of that structure.
--
--   The conclusion asserts the existence of a term $D$ of `FormalQuotientDatum 𝒪 π K₀ Onr Fr vdet r σ Γ Γ' g₁` — a tower of schemes $D.Y_n$ over $\mathcal O/\pi^{n+1}$ with cartesian transitions $D.yt_n$ and proper base maps $D.yb_n$, equipped with maps $D.q_n$ sending a pair (an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi:\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}\to B$, a Deligne datum $P$ over $B$), for $B$ a $\pi^{n+1}$-torsion $\mathcal O$-algebra, to a $B$-point of $D.Y_n$, compatible with the base, natural in $B$, compatible with the transitions, invariant under the twisted action of $\Gamma$, together with the fibre and the further axioms of that structure — satisfying in addition the following two conjuncts.
--
--   First, a coefficient law: for every $n$, every $\pi^{n+1}$-torsion $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$, all $\mathcal O$-algebra maps $\psi,\psi':\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}\to B$ and every Deligne datum $P$ over $B$, if $\psi$ and $\psi'$ agree on all $y$ with $\mathrm{Fr}(\mathrm{Fr}\,y)=y$, then $D.q_n(\psi,P)=D.q_n(\psi',P)$; that is, $D.q_n$ depends on $\psi$ only through its restriction to $\mathcal O_2$.
--
--   Second, an adic-point law. Let $C$ be an algebraically closed field, complete for a valuation with values in a linearly ordered commutative group with zero, equipped with a $K_0$-algebra structure; let $R$ be an $\mathcal O$-algebra with compatible maps to $C$ (scalar towers $\mathcal O\to R\to C$ and $\mathcal O\to K_0\to C$); let $\varpi$ be a pseudo-uniformiser of $K_0$ in $C$, i.e. an element of $K_0$ whose valuation in $C$ lies strictly between $0$ and $1$ and which scales all non-zero elements of $K_0$ by bounded powers. Assume `IsAdicFrame π ϖ R`: $\pi$ is irreducible, $R\to C$ is injective with image exactly the set of elements of valuation $\le 1$, $R$ is complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$, an element of $K_0$ has valuation $\le 1$ in $C$ precisely when it lies in the image of $\mathcal O$, the image of $K_0$ in $C$ is closed, and the images of $\pi$ and $\varpi$ in $C$ coincide. Let $\psi_0 : \mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}\to R$ be an $\mathcal O$-algebra map, and let $\mathrm{hmod}$ record that $\pi^{n+1}=0$ in $R/(\pi^{n+1})$. Writing $\bar\psi_0^{(n)}$ for $\psi_0$ followed by reduction modulo $\pi^{n+1}$, two assertions hold.
--
--   (a) For every family $\eta_n : \operatorname{Spec}(R/\pi^{n+1})\to D.Y_n$ which lies over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ (for each $n$, $\eta_n$ followed by $D.yb_n$ and then by $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\mathcal O\to\mathcal O/\pi^{n+1}$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\mathcal O\to R/\pi^{n+1}$) and is compatible with the transitions ($\operatorname{Spec}$ of the transition $R/\pi^{n+2}\to R/\pi^{n+1}$ followed by $\eta_{n+1}$ equals $\eta_n$ followed by $D.yt_n$), there is an adic point $x$ of $R$ — a compatible system of Deligne data $x.\mathrm{pt}_n$ over $R/\pi^{n+1}$, the datum at level $n+1$ pushing forward to that at level $n$ — with $\eta_n = D.q_n(\bar\psi_0^{(n)}, x.\mathrm{pt}_n)$ for all $n$.
--
--   (b) For adic points $x,x'$ of $R$, the equality $D.q_n(\bar\psi_0^{(n)},x.\mathrm{pt}_n) = D.q_n(\bar\psi_0^{(n)},x'.\mathrm{pt}_n)$ for all $n$ holds if and only if there is $\gamma\in\Gamma$ such that for every $n$ the pairs $(\bar\psi_0^{(n)},x.\mathrm{pt}_n)$ and $(\bar\psi_0^{(n)},x'.\mathrm{pt}_n)$ are related by the twisted action of $\sigma\gamma$ over $R/\pi^{n+1}$, i.e. the first component of the second pair is the Frobenius twist of the first component of the first pair by the exponent $-v\!\det(\sigma\gamma)$ (here both first components are $\bar\psi_0^{(n)}$) and $x'.\mathrm{pt}_n$ is the pullback of $x.\mathrm{pt}_n$ along $(\sigma\gamma)^{-1}$.
--
--   This is the assembly step in the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation: from Mumford's formal tower attached to a normal subgroup $N$ of finite index acting freely, together with the twisted tower over the quadratic unramified coefficient ring and a quotient of that tower by the finite group $\rho(\Gamma)/N$, it produces a formal quotient datum for the full group $\Gamma$, carrying both the coefficient law (dependence only on the $\mathrm{Fr}^2$-invariants) and the description of its adic points as $\Gamma$-orbits of compatible systems of Deligne data. It is cited by [`CerednikDrinfeld.exists_formalQuotientDatum_coeff_adicFib`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.exists_formalQuotientDatum_coeff_adicFib), and its proof invokes the descended quotient map of [`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalOmega.exists_descendedQuotientMap`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalOmega.exists_descendedQuotientMap) together with its universal property, its fibre and adic-point description, and its unramified-layer compatibility.
